  "answer": "### Short Answer\nThe deadline for filing an estate tax return is within one year from the decedent's death. An extension of up to 30 days may be granted by the Commissioner in meritorious cases.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **NIRC Sec. 89**: \"The estate tax return required... shall be filed within one (1) year from the decedent's death. The Commissioner shall have authority to grant, in meritorious cases, a reasonable extension not exceeding thirty (30) days for filing the return.\"\n\n### Interpretation\nThe law mandates that the estate tax return must be submitted within one year following the death of the individual whose estate is being settled. However, the Commissioner of Internal Revenue has the discretion to extend this deadline by an additional 30 days if there are valid reasons presented by the taxpayer.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis means that executors or heirs must ensure that the estate tax return is filed promptly to avoid penalties. If circumstances arise that make it difficult to meet the one-year deadline, they can request a short extension, but this must be justified to the satisfaction of the Commissioner.",
